\chapter{Command-Line Interface}
\label{cli-module}

The command line is Scholar's primary interface for non-interactive use.
While the TUI (\cref{tui-module}) provides an interactive review experience,
the CLI serves complementary purposes: quick searches, scripted workflows,
and pipeline integration.
A researcher might use the CLI to run a search, pipe the results to [[jq]] for
filtering, then export to BibTeX---all without leaving the shell.

The CLI exposes every major Scholar capability as a subcommand:
\begin{description}
\item[[scholar search]] Query multiple databases, with options for output
  format and interactive review.
\item[[scholar rq]] Start from a research question.
  Uses an LLM to generate provider-specific search queries, runs those searches
  with the same filter options as [[search]], and can label an initial batch of
  papers.
\item[[scholar sessions]] Manage saved review sessions: list, resume, export.
\item[[scholar providers]] Show available search providers and their
  configuration status.
\item[[scholar cache]] Inspect and clear the search result cache.
\item[[scholar pdf]] Download and manage paper PDFs.
\item[[scholar notes]] Access the notes database (list, search, export).
\item[[scholar themes]] Explore themes across review sessions.
\item[[scholar enrich]] Fetch missing abstracts for papers.
\item[[scholar llm]] Run LLM-assisted classification.
\end{description}

This chapter presents the implementation of each command.
We start with the core search commands ([[search]] and [[rq]]), then cover
utility commands (enrich, providers, cache, pdf, notes), session management,
and finally LLM-assisted review.


\section{Design decisions}

We use Typer for the CLI framework because it provides:
\begin{itemize}
\item Automatic help generation from type annotations
\item Subcommand support for modular organization
\item Integration with Rich for formatted output
\end{itemize}

The CLI is organized as subcommands to prepare for future extensions.
Currently, the core commands are [[search]] and [[rq]].
Later we will add [[snowball]] and [[tuxedo]] as git submodules that register
their own commands.


