\subsection{Executing the search}

We import the [[Search]] class from the core module and execute the query.
The results are then passed to the appropriate formatter.

Since providers automatically paginate to fetch results beyond their per-request
limits, users can request more than a single page of results.
The [[--limit/-l]] option controls the maximum results per provider, and
[[--limit 0]] fetches all available pages.
Large (or unlimited) searches may take time and consume API quotas, but the
provider implementations handle paging and rate limiting automatically:

<<execute search and display results>>=
from scholar import Search, SearchFilters, SearchResult
from scholar.providers import get_default_providers, expand_provider_names
import sys

# Determine which providers will be used. Group aliases (e.g.
# "preprints") are expanded here so the log and any session record
# name the actual per-server providers.
if provider is None:
    provider_names = [p.name for p in get_default_providers()]
else:
    provider_names = expand_provider_names(provider)

effective_limit = UNLIMITED_PROVIDER_LIMIT if limit == 0 else limit
limit_label = "unlimited" if limit == 0 else str(limit)

logger.info(f"Searching with providers: {', '.join(provider_names)}")
logger.debug(f"Search query: '{query}'")
logger.debug(f"Limit per provider: {limit_label}")

# Build search filters from CLI options
filters = None
if any([year, open_access, venue, min_citations is not None, pub_type]):
    filters = SearchFilters(
        year=year,
        open_access=open_access,
        venue=venue,
        min_citations=min_citations,
        pub_types=pub_type,
    )

s = Search(query)
results = s.execute(
    providers=None if provider is None else provider_names,
    limit=effective_limit,
    filters=filters,
)

logger.debug(f"Retrieved {len(results)} result sets")

# Merge results from all providers (deduplicates and consolidates data)
<<merge search results>>

# Optionally enrich papers with missing abstracts
<<enrich papers if requested>>

# If review mode, launch TUI instead of displaying results
if review:
    from scholar.tui import run_review
    session_name = name if name else query
    run_review(
        merged_results,
        query,
        session_name,
        prompt_report=report,
        search_filters=filters,
    )
    return

# An explicit -n without --review still records the search
if name:
    <<persist search to named session>>

# Auto-detect format based on TTY
actual_format = output_format
if output_format == "auto":
    actual_format = "table" if sys.stdout.isatty() else "csv"

display_results(merged_results, actual_format)
@

\subsection{Persisting named sessions without review}
\label{sec:search-session-persist}

An explicit [[-n]] always persists the search to the named review session,
whether or not the TUI runs.  Before this, [[-n]] without [[--review]] was
silently ignored: a scripted systematic review that ran [[scholar search -n
vt-terminal-systematic -f json]] per query got its output, but [[sessions
list]] showed nothing and [[sessions export]] failed---the audit trail
existed only for TUI users, and scripted runs had to reassemble the session
JSON by hand (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/66}).

We reuse [[create_review_session]], the same factory the TUI and [[rq]] use:
it loads any previous session with the same name, merges the accumulated
search records (query, provider, filters, limit per search), and preserves
earlier decisions while adding new papers as pending.  Repeating [[-n]] over
several searches therefore appends, exactly as the flag's help text always
promised, and decisions simply stay pending until made in the TUI, by the
LLM, or with [[sessions decide]] (\cref{sec:sessions-decide}).

We persist \emph{before} displaying: the session record is the point of
[[-n]], so if saving fails the command must fail loudly rather than emit
results that were never recorded.  The confirmation goes to stderr because
stdout may be machine-read ([[-f json]]/[[-f bibtex]] in pipelines) and must
stay pure.

<<persist search to named session>>=
from scholar.review import create_review_session, save_session

session = create_review_session(merged_results, query, session_name=name)
session_path = save_session(session)
Console(stderr=True).print(
    f"[green]Saved session:[/green] {escape(str(session_path))} "
    f"({len(session.decisions)} papers, "
    f"{len(session.pending_papers())} pending)"
)
@

Let's verify the flag actually persists: a fresh [[-n]] search creates a
loadable session with the papers pending, a repeated one appends instead of
duplicating, stdout stays pure JSON, and a search \emph{without} [[-n]]
still saves nothing:

<<test functions>>=
class TestSearchSessionPersist:
    """Tests for search -n persisting a session without --review."""

    def _mock_provider(self, monkeypatch, papers):
        from scholar import providers

        mock_provider = Mock()
        mock_provider.name = "mock"
        mock_provider.MAX_LIMIT = None
        mock_provider.search.return_value = papers
        monkeypatch.setattr(
            providers, "PROVIDERS", {"mock": mock_provider}
        )

    def test_name_persists_session(self, tmp_path, monkeypatch):
        """search -n saves a session with all papers pending."""
        from scholar.review import load_session

        monkeypatch.setenv("SCHOLAR_DATA_DIR", str(tmp_path))
        self._mock_provider(monkeypatch, [
            Paper(title="Persisted", authors=["A"], doi="10.1/p"),
        ])

        result = runner.invoke(
            app, ["search", "q1", "-n", "my-review", "-f", "json"]
        )
        assert result.exit_code == 0

        session = load_session("my-review")
        assert session is not None
        assert len(session.decisions) == 1
        assert len(session.pending_papers()) == 1
        assert session.search_records[0].provider == "mock"

    def test_repeated_name_appends(self, tmp_path, monkeypatch):
        """A second search with the same -n appends, not duplicates."""
