            for r in data["search_records"]
        )
        assert data["decisions"][0]["source"] == "llm"
@

\section{Reporting currency issues}
\label{sec:currency-reporting}

Both [[enrich]] and [[verify]] need to render a paper's currency problem the
same way---red for retractions, yellow for the milder corrections and
supersessions---so we factor the rendering into one helper rather than letting
the two commands drift apart.  The colour mapping keys off the status word from
[[currency_status]] (\cref{crossref-module}), so severity is decided in exactly
one place across the whole system.

We build a Rich [[Text]] object rather than a markup string because paper titles
and DOIs are untrusted data: appending them as plain segments means a title
containing [[[/red]]] cannot accidentally close our styling or inject markup.

The helper returns [[None]] for papers without a problem (clean, unchecked, or
DOI-less).  Returning a sentinel rather than printing lets each caller decide
what to do with the silent majority: [[enrich]] drops them, while [[verify]]
still lists them as \enquote{ok} or \enquote{unknown}.

<<currency reporting helpers>>=
from scholar.crossref import currency_status

CURRENCY_PROBLEM_STYLES = {
    "retracted": "bold red",
    "corrected": "yellow",
    "superseded": "yellow",
}


def format_currency_warning(paper) -> Optional[Text]:
    """Return a Rich Text warning for a paper with a currency issue.

    Returns None for papers that are ok, unchecked, or lack a DOI, so callers
    can skip the silent majority.
    """
    status = currency_status(paper)
    style = CURRENCY_PROBLEM_STYLES.get(status)
    if style is None:
        return None
    if status == "superseded":
        links = [v.get("doi") for v in (paper.newer_versions or [])]
    else:
        links = [n.get("doi") for n in (paper.updated_by or [])]
    links_str = ", ".join(doi for doi in links if doi) or "?"

    text = Text()
    text.append(f"[{status.upper()}] ", style=style)
    text.append(paper.title_preview())
    text.append(f"  -> {links_str}", style="dim")
    return text
@

Let's verify the helper styles and skips correctly:

