\section{Enriching Papers}

The [[enrich]] command fetches missing abstracts/metadata for papers in a
review session.  This is useful when you've already done a search and want to
fill in abstracts without re-running the search.  It is also where a session
learns which of its papers are open access: the enrichment step consults
Unpaywall (\cref{unpaywall-module}) for every paper with a DOI, and the
command reports the tally.

<<enrich command>>=
@app.command()
def enrich(
    session: Annotated[
        str,
        typer.Argument(
            help="Name of the review session to enrich.",
            autocompletion=complete_session_name,
        ),
    ],
    enrich_providers: Annotated[
        Optional[str],
        typer.Option(
            "--enrich-providers",
            help=(
                "Comma-separated provider names to use for enrichment. "
                "Overrides the default order."
            ),
        ),
    ] = None,
    preprint_search: Annotated[
        bool,
        typer.Option(
            "--preprint-search",
            help=(
                "When no PDF is found, also search preprint servers by "
                "title and authors for a preprint version to adopt. "
                "Authoritative preprint links (OpenAlex, Crossref) are "
                "followed even without this flag."
            ),
        ),
    ] = False,
) -> None:
    """
    Fetch missing abstracts/metadata for papers in a review session.

    Looks up papers by DOI in available providers to fill in missing
    abstracts/metadata. Providers requiring API keys (IEEE, WoS) are used
    only when configured. Also looks up open-access status and PDF links
    via Unpaywall (requires SCHOLAR_EMAIL) and checks Crossref for
    retractions. When no PDF can be found, a preprint version may be
    adopted in place of the published entry (see --preprint-search).

    Example:
        scholar enrich "machine learning"
    """
    <<enrich session papers>>
@

\subsection{Enriching session papers}

We load the session, enrich the papers, and save the updated session.
Enrichment runs over \emph{all} the session's papers, not only those missing
an abstract: a paper can be metadata-complete yet never have had its
open-access status looked up, and the per-paper cost for a complete paper is
one cached (or skipped) Unpaywall call.  The \enquote{N papers to enrich}
message therefore describes the metadata gaps, while the open-access and
currency passes below always cover the whole session.

<<enrich session papers>>=
from scholar.review import load_session, save_session
from scholar.enrich import enrich_papers, needs_enrichment
from scholar import unpaywall
from rich.progress import Progress

console = Console()

# Load the session
session_data = load_session(session)
if session_data is None:
    console.print(f"Session not found: {escape(session)}")
    raise typer.Exit(1)

# Extract papers from decisions
papers = [decision.paper for decision in session_data.decisions]
enrich_fields = ["abstract", "pdf_url", "keywords"]
papers_to_enrich = [p for p in papers if needs_enrichment(p, enrich_fields)]

providers_for_enrich = (
    [p.strip() for p in enrich_providers.split(",") if p.strip()]
    if enrich_providers
    else None
)

if papers_to_enrich:
    console.print(f"[cyan]Found {len(papers_to_enrich)} papers to enrich (of {len(papers)} total).[/cyan]")
else:
    console.print("[yellow]No papers need metadata enrichment (all have abstracts).[/yellow]")

# Enrich with progress bar (all papers, so open-access status is filled too)
with Progress() as progress:
    task = progress.add_task("[cyan]Enriching...", total=len(papers))

    def update_progress(current: int, total: int) -> None:
        progress.update(task, completed=current)

    enriched_papers = enrich_papers(
        papers,
        fields=enrich_fields,
        providers=providers_for_enrich,
        progress_callback=update_progress,
        preprint_search=preprint_search,
    )

# Update decisions with enriched papers (preserve order)
for decision, enriched in zip(session_data.decisions, enriched_papers):
    decision.paper = enriched

<<record preprint adoptions during enrich>>

<<report open-access findings during enrich>>

<<warn on currency issues during enrich>>

save_session(session_data)

# Count how many gained an abstract or a PDF link
enriched_count = sum(
    1 for orig, new in zip(papers, enriched_papers)
    if (new.abstract and orig.abstract != new.abstract)
    or (new.pdf_url and orig.pdf_url != new.pdf_url)
)
console.print(f"[green]Enriched {enriched_count} papers.[/green]")
@

When the preprint fallback (\cref{preprint-module}) adopted a preprint,
the paper's identity changed---and the notes and decision stores are
keyed by that identity, so records saved under the published DOI would
be orphaned.  Adoption is detectable by comparing ids across the
enrichment: [[enrich_paper]] is the only enrichment step that can
change [[Paper.id]].  For each adoption we migrate the persistent
stores ([[migrate_paper_id]], \cref{notes-module}) and tell the user
what was swapped, naming both DOIs so the substitution is transparent
in the terminal transcript, not only in the session file.

<<record preprint adoptions during enrich>>=
from scholar.notes import get_paper_id, migrate_paper_id

for orig, new in zip(papers, enriched_papers):
    if get_paper_id(orig) == get_paper_id(new):
        continue
    migrate_paper_id(get_paper_id(orig), get_paper_id(new))
    console.print(
        f"[cyan]Adopted preprint {new.doi} for "
        f"\N{LEFT DOUBLE QUOTATION MARK}{escape(new.title_preview())}"
        f"\N{RIGHT DOUBLE QUOTATION MARK} "
        f"(published DOI {orig.doi} kept for provenance).[/cyan]"
    )
@

The open-access tally answers the question a reviewer actually has---how much
of this session can I read?---so we count open papers and, among them, those
with a direct PDF link, over the papers Unpaywall managed to check.  When no
contact email is configured the lookups were silently skipped, and the only
useful thing to say is which variable enables them; we say it once here rather
than once per paper.

<<report open-access findings during enrich>>=
if unpaywall.is_configured():
    checked = [p for p in enriched_papers if p.oa_status is not None]
    open_papers = [p for p in checked if p.is_open_access]
    with_pdf = [p for p in open_papers if p.pdf_url]
    console.print(
        f"[cyan]Open access: {len(open_papers)} of {len(checked)} checked "
        f"papers ({len(with_pdf)} with PDF link).[/cyan]"
    )
else:
    console.print(
        "[dim]Unpaywall lookup skipped: set SCHOLAR_EMAIL to enable "
        "open-access checks.[/dim]"
    )
@

Enrichment is also the natural moment to check currency: we already hold the
session's papers and are about to write them back, so a retraction check costs
one extra pass and saves its results in the same [[save_session]].  We check
\emph{every} paper, not just those that needed metadata enrichment---a paper can
be complete yet retracted---so this runs over all decisions rather than the
[[papers_to_enrich]] subset.

We only surface problems here (a warning per retracted, corrected, or
superseded paper); the clean and unchecked majority stay silent.  Users who want
the full per-paper picture run [[scholar verify]] instead.

<<warn on currency issues during enrich>>=
from scholar.crossref import annotate_papers

console.print(
    "[cyan]Checking Crossref for retractions/corrections...[/cyan]"
)
checked = annotate_papers([d.paper for d in session_data.decisions])
for decision, paper in zip(session_data.decisions, checked):
    decision.paper = paper

warnings = [
    text
    for paper in checked
    if (text := format_currency_warning(paper)) is not None
]
if warnings:
    console.print(f"[red]Found {len(warnings)} currency issue(s):[/red]")
    for text in warnings:
        console.print(text)
else:
    console.print("[green]No retractions or corrections found.[/green]")
@


\section{Verifying currency}
\label{sec:verify-command}

Where [[enrich]] checks currency as a side effect, [[verify]] makes it the
point: it reports the status of \emph{every} paper in a session, not just the
problem ones, and offers a [[--json]] mode so the [[backing-claims]] skill can
consume the result programmatically.  It shares [[enrich]]'s argument shape (a
session name with the same completion) so the two commands feel like a pair.

<<verify command>>=
@app.command()
def verify(
    session: Annotated[
        str,
        typer.Argument(
            help="Name of the review session to check.",
            autocompletion=complete_session_name,
        ),
    ],
    json_output: Annotated[
        bool,
        typer.Option(
            "--json",
            help="Emit the currency report as a JSON array.",
        ),
    ] = False,
) -> None:
    """
    Check a session's papers for retractions, corrections, and newer versions.

    Looks up each paper's DOI in Crossref and reports whether it has been
    retracted or corrected, or has a superseding version. The discovered
    status is saved back to the session.

    Example:
        scholar verify "machine learning"
    """
    <<verify session currency>>
@

We borrow the stderr-console discipline introduced for [[pdf quote]]
(\cref{ch:pdf-cli}): all progress and diagnostics go to [[err_console]], so
in [[--json]] mode standard output carries nothing but the JSON array and a
consumer can pipe it straight into a parser.  A missing session is therefore an
error on stderr plus a non-zero exit, never a stray line on stdout.

<<verify session currency>>=
from scholar.review import load_session, save_session
from scholar.crossref import annotate_papers, currency_status
from rich.progress import Progress

console = Console()
err_console = Console(stderr=True)

session_data = load_session(session)
if session_data is None:
    err_console.print(f"[red]Session not found:[/red] {escape(session)}")
    raise typer.Exit(1)

papers = [decision.paper for decision in session_data.decisions]
<<run the currency check with a progress bar>>

# Persist the discovered status back to the session.
for decision, paper in zip(session_data.decisions, checked):
    decision.paper = paper
save_session(session_data)

<<output the currency report>>
@

The progress bar is attached to [[err_console]] for the same reason: a Rich
progress display writes escape sequences that must not contaminate JSON output.
The per-paper callback drives it as Crossref is queried one DOI at a time.

<<run the currency check with a progress bar>>=
with Progress(console=err_console) as progress:
    task = progress.add_task(
        "[cyan]Checking Crossref...", total=len(papers)
    )

    def update_progress(done: int, total: int) -> None:
        progress.update(task, completed=done)

    checked = annotate_papers(papers, progress_callback=update_progress)
@

The JSON shape mirrors what a verification checklist needs: the DOI and title to
identify the paper, the one-word [[status]], and the raw notice and
newer-version lists so a caller can show the linking DOIs without a second
lookup.  [[published_versions]] is included so an adopted preprint's
provenance (which published DOI it replaced) travels with its
\enquote{preprint} status.  An empty session is a valid empty array,
not a message.

<<output the currency report>>=
if json_output:
    report = [
        {
            "doi": paper.doi,
            "title": paper.title,
            "status": currency_status(paper),
            "updated_by": paper.updated_by,
            "newer_versions": paper.newer_versions,
            "published_versions": paper.published_versions,
        }
        for paper in checked
    ]
    print(json.dumps(report, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2))
    return

<<print the human currency report>>
@

In human mode we lead with the problems---each rendered by the shared
[[format_currency_warning]] helper---and close with a one-line tally of every
status so the reader can see the clean and unchecked counts too.
Between the two sit the adopted preprints: they are deliberate
substitutions, not problems, so they render as dim informational lines
(\enquote{preprint of \dots}, naming the published DOI) rather than
warnings, and get their own slot in the tally.

<<print the human currency report>>=
from collections import Counter

problems = [
    text
    for paper in checked
    if (text := format_currency_warning(paper)) is not None
]
if problems:
    for text in problems:
        console.print(text)
else:
    console.print(
        "[green]No retractions, corrections, or newer versions found.[/green]"
    )

for paper in checked:
    if currency_status(paper) != "preprint":
        continue
    published_dois = ", ".join(
        entry["doi"]
        for entry in paper.published_versions or []
        if entry.get("doi")
    )
    console.print(
        f"[dim]\\[PREPRINT] {escape(paper.title_preview())} "
        f"-> preprint of {published_dois}[/dim]"
    )

counts = Counter(currency_status(paper) for paper in checked)
console.print(
    f"[bold]{len(checked)} papers[/bold]: "
    f"{counts.get('retracted', 0)} retracted, "
    f"{counts.get('corrected', 0)} corrected, "
    f"{counts.get('superseded', 0)} superseded, "
    f"{counts.get('preprint', 0)} preprint, "
    f"{counts.get('ok', 0)} ok, "
    f"{counts.get('unknown', 0)} unknown"
)
@

Let's verify the command end to end: the human report, the JSON mode, and the
missing-session error.  We build a small session on disk and stub the Crossref
lookup so no network is touched---the stub marks one paper retracted and leaves
the other clean.

<<test functions>>=
class TestEnrichCommand:
