\section{Listing and Checking Providers}

Users need to know which search providers are available, how to configure
them---and whether that configuration actually \emph{works}.
Rather than requiring users to read the literate documentation, we provide a
[[providers]] command that displays this information directly.

Following the cache command's action-based design (\cref{ch:cache-cli}), the
command takes an action argument rather than subcommands.  The action
defaults to [[list]] so the long-standing bare [[scholar providers]]
invocation keeps working; [[check]] adds the health probe, and [[limits]]
shows the persistent rate-limit bookkeeping (\cref{ch:ratelimit}).

<<providers command>>=
@app.command()
def providers(
    action: Annotated[
        str,
        typer.Argument(
            help="Action: list, check, or limits",
            autocompletion=complete_providers_action,
        ),
    ] = "list",
    offline: Annotated[
        bool,
        typer.Option(
            "--offline",
            help="Only check configuration; make no network requests.",
        ),
    ] = False,
    reset: Annotated[
        Optional[str],
        typer.Option(
            "--reset",
            help="With limits: clear persisted rate-limit state for the"
            " named provider, or for every provider with 'all'.",
        ),
    ] = None,
) -> None:
    """
    List available search providers and their configuration.

    Actions:
        list   - Show providers, API key requirements, how to get keys
        check  - Probe each provider; report key and connectivity problems
        limits - Show rate limits, quota usage, and reset times
    """
    if action == "list":
        <<display provider information>>
    elif action == "check":
        <<check provider health>>
    elif action == "limits":
        <<display provider limits>>
    else:
        console = Console()
        console.print(f"[red]Unknown action: {escape(action)}[/red]")
        console.print("Valid actions: list, check, limits")
        raise typer.Exit(1)
@

\subsection{Checking provider health}

The check action exists because a misconfigured provider fails
\emph{silently} during searches: every provider degrades to an empty result
list on error, so an expired API key just makes its provider contribute
nothing (see the health-check section of the providers module for the
review that this actually happened to).  Running [[scholar providers check]]
before a big search surfaces the problem while it is still cheap to fix.

The exit code makes the command usable as a pre-flight gate in scripts.  We
exit nonzero if and only if a \emph{configured} provider fails---a rejected
key, an HTTP error, or a network error.  \enquote{Not configured} is a normal
state (most users have no Scopus key) and exiting nonzero on it would make
the gate useless on minimal setups.  \enquote{Rate limited} also exits
zero: the credentials and connectivity are fine, and the search path already
handles 429s with back-off, so failing on one would make the gate flaky.
The same reasoning covers \enquote{quota exhausted}: the configuration is
fine, the budget is simply spent, and the search path now skips such a
provider with a warning (\cref{ch:ratelimit})---which also means the
check reports it from the persisted state \emph{without} burning a probe
request against the spent budget.
Nonzero thus means exactly \enquote{a provider you believe you have will
silently contribute nothing}.

<<check provider health>>=
from scholar.providers import (
    HEALTH_HTTP_ERROR,
    HEALTH_KEY_REJECTED,
    HEALTH_NETWORK_ERROR,
    HEALTH_OK,
    HEALTH_QUOTA_EXHAUSTED,
    HEALTH_RATE_LIMITED,
    check_provider,
    get_all_providers,
)

console = Console()

status_styles = {
    HEALTH_OK: "green",
    HEALTH_KEY_REJECTED: "red",
    HEALTH_HTTP_ERROR: "red",
    HEALTH_NETWORK_ERROR: "red",
    HEALTH_RATE_LIMITED: "yellow",
    HEALTH_QUOTA_EXHAUSTED: "yellow",
}
failure_statuses = (
    HEALTH_KEY_REJECTED,
    HEALTH_HTTP_ERROR,
    HEALTH_NETWORK_ERROR,
)

table = Table(title="Provider Health", box=box.HORIZONTALS)
table.add_column("Provider", style="cyan")
table.add_column("Configured", style="magenta")
table.add_column("Status")
table.add_column("Detail", style="dim")

failures = []
for provider in get_all_providers():
    health = check_provider(provider, live=not offline)
    style = status_styles.get(health.status, "dim")
    configured = (
        "[green]Yes[/green]" if health.configured else "[dim]No[/dim]"
    )
    table.add_row(
        health.name,
        configured,
        f"[{style}]{health.status}[/{style}]",
        health.message,
    )
    if health.configured and health.status in failure_statuses:
        failures.append(health)

console.print(table)
if failures:
    console.print()
    for health in failures:
        console.print(
            f"[red]{health.name}: {health.status}[/red] {health.message}"
        )
    raise typer.Exit(1)
@

\subsection{Showing rate limits and quota usage}

The limits action surfaces the persistent rate-limit bookkeeping of
\cref{ch:ratelimit}: declared limits, how much of today's budget each
provider has absorbed, and when a spent budget comes back.
One row per \emph{quota group}, not per provider---the eight
OpenAlex-backed providers share one budget and appear as one
[[openalex]] row, which is the truthful unit of accounting.

The [[--reset]] escape hatch deletes the persisted state.
It exists because the bookkeeping is deliberately conservative: the
daily reset time is a guess (\cref{ch:ratelimit}), and a replaced API
key starts a fresh budget the file knows nothing about.
When the user \emph{knows} the quota is back, resetting beats waiting.

<<display provider limits>>=
from scholar import ratelimit

console = Console()

if reset is not None:
    count = ratelimit.reset_state(None if reset == "all" else reset)
    console.print(f"Cleared {count} rate-limit state file(s).")
    raise typer.Exit(0)

table = Table(title="Provider Rate Limits", box=box.HORIZONTALS)
table.add_column("Provider", style="cyan", no_wrap=True)
table.add_column("Limits")
table.add_column("Used today", justify="right")
table.add_column("Remaining", justify="right")
table.add_column("Resets")
table.add_column("Status")

for status in ratelimit.all_status():
    <<format one limits row>>

console.print(table)
@

Each row folds the availability judgement into a colour-coded status:
green for available, red for a spent quota (act tomorrow, or
[[--reset]]), yellow for a transient cooldown (just wait).
The \enquote{Remaining} column prefers the provider's own report
(synced from [[X-RateLimit-*]] headers) over our local subtraction,
matching the limiter's own order of authority.

<<format one limits row>>=
limits_parts = []
if status.limits.per_second:
    limits_parts.append(f"{status.limits.per_second:g}/s")
if status.limits.daily:
    limits_parts.append(f"{status.limits.daily}/day")
if status.remaining is not None:
    remaining_text = str(status.remaining)
elif status.limits.daily:
    remaining_text = str(max(0, status.limits.daily - status.day_count))
else:
    remaining_text = "--"
availability = status.availability
if availability.ok:
    status_text = "[green]ok[/green]"
elif availability.quota:
    status_text = "[red]quota exhausted[/red]"
else:
    status_text = "[yellow]cooling down[/yellow]"
table.add_row(
    status.name,
    ", ".join(limits_parts) or "--",
    str(status.day_count),
    remaining_text,
    ratelimit.format_retry(status.reset_at)
    if status.reset_at
    else "--",
    status_text,
)
@

We organize provider information in a table showing the provider name, whether
it is configured, whether an API key is required, and the environment variable
to set.

The status column reports [[is_available()]]---true when the provider needs no
key or already has one in the environment, the very predicate
[[get_default_providers]] uses to choose what a bare search queries.  We label
it \enquote{Configured} rather than \enquote{Default} because the two status
columns were being read together and misread: a red \enquote{Required} in the
API-key column looked like \emph{this key is missing}, even on rows where
\enquote{Default: Yes} had already said the key was present.  Naming the
column after the question it actually answers---is this provider ready to
use?---removes that ambiguity, and it matches the identically-named column in
[[providers check]] above, so the same word means the same thing in both
tables.

For the same reason we no longer paint \enquote{Required} red
unconditionally.  Red is how this CLI marks something the user must act
on---[[providers check]] uses it for a rejected key---so spending it on a
requirement that is already satisfied trains readers to ignore it.  Red now
appears only when a required key is genuinely absent, which is exactly the row
that needs attention.

The longer header costs horizontal space, and at the 80-column default that
pushed [[WOS_EXPANDED_API_KEY]] over its column's width.  Rich's default
overflow ellipsized it to \enquote{WOS\_EXPANDED\_API\_K\ldots}, which is
worse than useless in the two columns the reader is meant to \emph{copy}: an
environment variable or a URL cut mid-token cannot be acted on.  The
prose-like \enquote{How to Get Key} column folds, so a value that does not
fit wraps rather than disappearing.

The variable column cannot rely on folding plus a [[min_width]] floor,
though that was the first design.  Rich (15.0.0) resolves contention by
shrinking foldable columns and then \emph{re-measuring} each column, and
the re-measure clamps back to [[min_width]]---after the shrinking is
already done.  With enough contention (our fifteen rows provide it) the
clamped total exceeds the console width, and Rich then crops every
rendered line to fit, silently dropping the trailing characters of the
last column: exactly the mid-token loss the floor was meant to prevent,
minus even the ellipsis that would reveal it
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/74}).

So the copy-material columns negotiate nothing: the provider-name and
variable columns are [[no_wrap]], and the one cell listing alternative
variables carries explicit line breaks
(\cref{sec:provider-config-reference}).  A [[no_wrap]] column self-sizes
to its longest line and is exempt from shrinking, so those names always
arrive intact---all contention lands on the foldable prose column, which
wraps harmlessly---and adding a provider with a longer name or variable
widens its column instead of splitting or ellipsizing the name.
The table also drops its edge padding ([[pad_edge=False]]): with four
columns now rigid, those two blank columns of margin are what let
\enquote{No key needed} render on one line at 80 columns.

<<display provider information>>=
from scholar.providers import get_all_providers, PROVIDER_GROUPS

console = Console()

<<provider configuration reference>>

table = Table(
    title="Available Search Providers",
    box=box.HORIZONTALS,
    pad_edge=False,
)
table.add_column("Provider", style="cyan", no_wrap=True)
