\section{PDF Management}
\label{ch:pdf-cli}

Researchers often need to download and view PDFs when reviewing papers.
The [[pdf]] command provides access to PDF download, caching, and viewing
functionality.

\subsection{Command Design}

Like the [[cache]] command, [[pdf]] uses an action argument with an optional
URL parameter:
\begin{description}
\item[pdf open <url>] Download (if needed) and open a PDF from its URL
\item[pdf info] Show PDF cache statistics
\item[pdf clear] Delete all cached PDFs
\item[pdf path] Print the PDF cache directory path
\item[ pdf quote <url> [[--claim]] <claim> ] Surface candidate supporting
  passages for a claim
\end{description}

The [[open]] action is the primary use case---it handles the full workflow
of checking the cache, downloading if necessary, and opening in the system
viewer.

The [[quote]] action supports the verification step of citing a source: given a
claim, it prints candidate passages and the page each came from, leaving the
judgement to the reader (see \cref{sec:quote-extraction}).
Its options ([[--claim]], [[--limit]], [[--model]], [[--json]], and the
[[--lexical]]/[[--llm]] stage toggles) apply only to this action;
they sit on the shared [[pdf]] command because, like [[cache]], it dispatches on
a single action argument rather than splitting into sub-commands.
We print results with the builtin [[print]] so a reader can pipe the passages
into other tools, and route progress and warnings to a stderr console so they
never contaminate that output.

<<pdf command>>=
@app.command()
def pdf(
    action: Annotated[
        str,
        typer.Argument(
            help="Action: open, info, clear, path, or quote",
            autocompletion=complete_pdf_action,
        ),
    ],
    url: Annotated[
        Optional[str],
        typer.Argument(help="PDF URL (required for 'open' and 'quote')"),
    ] = None,
    <<pdf quote options>>
) -> None:
    """
    Manage PDF downloads and cache.

    Actions:
        open <url>   - Download (if needed) and open a PDF
        info         - Show PDF cache statistics
        clear        - Delete all cached PDFs
        path         - Print the PDF cache directory path
        quote <url>  - Surface candidate passages supporting --claim

    Examples:
        scholar pdf open "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2301.00001.pdf"
        scholar pdf info
        scholar pdf clear
        scholar pdf quote "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2301.00001.pdf" \\
            --claim "AE provides confidentiality and integrity"
    """
    from scholar.pdf import (
        PDFDownloadError,
        clear_pdf_cache,
        find_quote_candidates,
        get_pdf,
        get_pdf_cache_dir,
        get_pdf_cache_info,
        open_pdf,
    )

    console = Console()

    if action == "open":
        if not url:
            console.print("[red]Error: URL required for 'open' action[/red]")
            console.print("Usage: scholar pdf open <url>")
            raise typer.Exit(1)
        
        try:
            console.print(f"[dim]Fetching PDF...[/dim]")
            pdf_path = get_pdf(url)
            console.print(
                f"[green]PDF cached at: {escape(str(pdf_path))}[/green]"
            )
            
            if open_pdf(pdf_path):
                console.print("[dim]Opened in system viewer[/dim]")
            else:
                console.print("[yellow]Could not open PDF viewer[/yellow]")
                console.print(
                    f"[dim]PDF available at: {escape(str(pdf_path))}[/dim]"
                )
        except PDFDownloadError as e:
            console.print(f"[red]Download failed: {escape(str(e))}[/red]")
            raise typer.Exit(1)

    elif action == "info":
        info = get_pdf_cache_info()
        table = Table(title="PDF Cache Statistics", box=box.HORIZONTALS)
        table.add_column("Metric", style="cyan")
        table.add_column("Value", style="green")

        table.add_row("Location", str(get_pdf_cache_dir()))
        table.add_row("Cached PDFs", str(info["count"]))
        size_mb = info["size_bytes"] / (1024 * 1024)
        table.add_row("Total size", f"{size_mb:.1f} MB")

        console.print(table)

    elif action == "clear":
        count = clear_pdf_cache()
        console.print(f"[green]Cleared {count} cached PDF(s).[/green]")

    elif action == "path":
        print(get_pdf_cache_dir())

    elif action == "quote":
        <<pdf quote action>>

    else:
        console.print(f"[red]Unknown action: {escape(action)}[/red]")
        console.print("Valid actions: open, info, clear, path, quote")
        raise typer.Exit(1)
@

\subsection{The quote action}

The options that drive [[quote]] are deferred to a placeholder in the command
signature so the shared parts of [[pdf]] stay readable.
The two stage toggles use Typer's [[--flag/--no-flag]] form so each defaults to
on yet can be turned off independently.

<<pdf quote options>>=
claim: Annotated[
    Optional[str],
    typer.Option(
        "--claim",
        help="Claim to find supporting passages for (required for 'quote').",
    ),
] = None,
quote_limit: Annotated[
    int,
    typer.Option(
        "--limit", "-n",
        help="Number of candidate passages to show (quote).",
    ),
] = 5,
quote_model: Annotated[
    Optional[str],
    typer.Option(
        "--model", "-m",
        help="LLM model for reranking quotes (uses llm default if unset).",
        autocompletion=complete_model,
    ),
] = None,
use_lexical: Annotated[
    bool,
    typer.Option(
        "--lexical/--no-lexical",
        help="Toggle the lexical ranking stage (quote).",
    ),
] = True,
use_llm: Annotated[
    bool,
    typer.Option(
        "--llm/--no-llm",
        help="Toggle the LLM rerank stage (quote).",
    ),
] = True,
json_output: Annotated[
    bool,
    typer.Option(
        "--json",
        help="Emit candidates as a JSON array (quote).",
    ),
] = False,
@

We validate the same way the [[open]] action does---a missing URL or claim is a
usage error---and additionally reject disabling both ranking stages, since then
there is nothing to rank.

<<pdf quote action>>=
if not url:
    console.print("[red]Error: URL required for 'quote' action[/red]")
    console.print('Usage: scholar pdf quote <url> --claim "..."')
    raise typer.Exit(1)
if not claim:
    console.print("[red]Error: --claim required for 'quote' action[/red]")
    raise typer.Exit(1)
if not use_lexical and not use_llm:
    console.print(
        "[red]Error: enable at least one of --lexical / --llm[/red]"
    )
    raise typer.Exit(1)

err_console = Console(stderr=True)
try:
    candidates = find_quote_candidates(
        url,
        claim,
        limit=quote_limit,
        model_id=quote_model,
        use_lexical=use_lexical,
        use_llm=use_llm,
        progress_callback=lambda msg: err_console.print(
            f"[dim]{escape(msg)}[/dim]"
        ),
    )
except PDFDownloadError as e:
    console.print(f"[red]Download failed: {escape(str(e))}[/red]")
    raise typer.Exit(1)

<<output quote candidates>>
@

The default output is one human-readable line per candidate.
A [[--json]] array is offered for programmatic consumers---notably the
[[backing-claims]] skill, which records a citation's provenance and wants the
page, score, and relevance as structured fields rather than parsing prose.
In JSON mode an empty result is a valid empty array (not a message), so a
caller can consume it unconditionally; the human mode instead prints a notice
to stderr.
This composes with the earlier decision to route all progress and warnings to a
stderr console: together they keep stdout pure JSON, so a consumer can pipe the
command straight into a JSON parser without filtering out diagnostics or
special-casing the no-results case.

<<output quote candidates>>=
if json_output:
    import json as _json
    from dataclasses import asdict

    print(
        _json.dumps(
            [asdict(candidate) for candidate in candidates],
            ensure_ascii=False,
            indent=2,
        )
    )
    return

if not candidates:
    err_console.print("[yellow]No candidate passages found.[/yellow]")
    return

for candidate in candidates:
    <<print a quote candidate>>
@

Each line gives the page, an optional relevance label when the LLM ranked it,
and the verbatim passage in quotes---close to the sketch in the issue.

<<print a quote candidate>>=
location = f"p.{candidate.page}"
if candidate.relevance:
    print(f'{location}  [{candidate.relevance}]  "{candidate.text}"')
else:
    print(f'{location}  "{candidate.text}"')
