\section{Citation Provenance}
\label{sec:prov-commands}

When a citation is backed by a paper, its provenance record needs a
[[FOUND-VIA]] line: a command that reproduces the search which surfaced the
paper.  A [[scholar rq]] session already records, per provider, the query it
sent to each database (in [[query_provider_pairs]]).  But [[rq]] generates those
queries with a language model, so re-running [[scholar rq]] on the same research
question is \emph{not} reproducible---a later run may phrase the queries
differently and return a different set.  The artifact that \emph{is}
reproducible is the exact query that was actually sent, which the session has
already persisted.  So [[found-via]] rebuilds a [[scholar search]] command from
that stored query rather than re-deriving it through the model, and demotes the
research question to human-readable context.

The lookup runs backwards from the one piece of information the author has at
citation time---a paper ID.  There is no index from a paper back to the sessions
that contain it, so [[found-via]] scans the saved sessions and reports every one
that returned the paper (a paper may have been found in more than one search).
We report it regardless of the keep/discard decision: provenance records
\emph{how} a paper surfaced, which is independent of whether the reviewer later
kept it, and an author may want the trail for a paper still under consideration.

\subsection{The prov command group}

We expose provenance under its own [[prov]] command group, mirroring the
[[sessions]] and [[notes]] groups.  This one group hosts both the read-only
[[found-via]] export described next and the [[import]]/[[export]] round-trip
between [[.bib]] files and notes built in \cref{sec:prov-roundtrip}; defining it
here, before either set of subcommands, lets all of them attach to the same
[[prov_app]].

<<prov command>>=
prov_app = typer.Typer(
    name="prov",
    help="Export and round-trip citation provenance.",
    no_args_is_help=True,
)
app.add_typer(prov_app, name="prov")
@

\subsection{Reconstructing a reproducible search}

The record for one paper in one session pins the command to the single provider
that actually returned the paper ([[decision.provider]]): re-running every
provider's query would reproduce the whole session, not the one result we are
citing.  We look that provider's whole search up via [[search_record_for]],
which gives back the query along with the filters and limit that were applied;
those become the same flags the [[bibtex+prov]] formatter emits, via the shared
[[_search_flags]] helper, so the command reproduces the \emph{exact} search and
not a broader one.  Sessions saved before per-provider records existed have
none, so we fall back to the session's overall [[query]] with no filters, which
is the best the older format can offer.

The research question travels along as [[research_context]] for the human
reader, but it never shapes the command.  We deliberately do \emph{not} key the
command on whether [[research_context]] is set: it can be filled in on any
session to drive automated classification, so it is not a marker of how the
session was built.

<<prov command>>=
def _found_via_record(session, decision) -> dict:
    """Build the reproducible provenance record for one paper.

    The command re-sends the exact provider query, filters, and limit stored
    for the paper's provider, reproducing the search without re-invoking the
    language model that ``scholar rq`` used to generate that query.
    """
    provider = decision.provider
    record = session.search_record_for(provider)
    provider_query = record.query if record else session.query
    filters = record.filters if record else None
    limit = record.limit if record else None
    parts = [f"scholar search {_quote_arg(provider_query)} -p {provider}"]
    parts += _search_flags(filters, limit)
    return {
        "paper_id": decision.paper.id,
        "title": decision.paper.title,
        "session": session.name or session.query,
        "command": " ".join(parts),
        "provider": provider,
        "provider_query": provider_query,
        "filters": filters,
        "limit": limit,
        "research_context": session.research_context,
        "timestamp": session.timestamp.isoformat(),
    }
@

\subsection{The found-via command}

The command resolves a paper ID the same way [[notes show]] does---exact match,
or substring so a bare DOI matches the [[doi:]]-prefixed ID---and follows the
sub-app [[--format]] convention so tooling can ask for JSON.  We walk the
sessions newest-first (the natural reading order for provenance: the most recent
search comes first) and collect every decision whose paper matches.

Resolving by substring is convenient but can be \emph{ambiguous}: a short
fragment may match two unrelated papers, and emitting provenance for both would
silently attribute the wrong search to a citation.  So we sharpen the match
before reporting.  An exact ID always wins over looser substring hits.  Failing
that, if the fragment still spans more than one distinct paper we refuse to
guess and ask for a full ID rather than print blocks the author cannot tell
apart.

The error paths respect [[--format]] too: JSON output must always be parseable,
so the not-found and ambiguous cases emit an empty array on [[stdout]] (with the
non-zero exit signalling the failure) instead of the human-readable message a
table reader gets.

<<prov command>>=
@prov_app.command("found-via")
def prov_found_via(
    paper_id: Annotated[
        str,
        typer.Argument(
            help="Paper ID (DOI or hash) to trace.",
            autocompletion=complete_session_paper_id,
        ),
    ],
    output_format: Annotated[
        str,
        typer.Option(
            "--format", "-f",
            help="Output format: table or json.",
            autocompletion=complete_display_format,
        ),
    ] = "table",
) -> None:
    """
    Print a reproducible FOUND-VIA provenance line for a paper.

    Scans saved review sessions for the paper and, for each session that
    returned it, prints the 'scholar search' command that reproduces the
    search which surfaced it. Use '-f json' for machine-readable output.
    """
    from scholar.review import list_sessions, load_session

    console = Console()
    <<collect matching decisions for the paper>>
    <<resolve the matches to a single paper>>
    records = [_found_via_record(s, d) for s, d in matches]

    if not records:
        if output_format == "json":
            print("[]")
        else:
            console.print(
                f"[red]No saved session contains paper ID: "
                f"{escape(paper_id)}[/red]"
            )
        raise typer.Exit(1)

    if output_format == "json":
        print(json.dumps(records, indent=2))
    else:
        <<print FOUND-VIA blocks>>
@

The scan loads each session by name and matches every decision's paper,
remembering the distinct IDs hit so we can detect ambiguity afterwards.  A
session that fails to load is skipped rather than fatal, so one corrupt file
does not hide the provenance held in the others.

<<collect matching decisions for the paper>>=
matches = []  # (session, decision) pairs, newest session first
matched_ids = set()
for meta in reversed(list_sessions()):
    session = load_session(meta["name"])
    if session is None:
        continue
    for decision in session.decisions:
        pid = decision.paper.id
        if pid == paper_id or paper_id in pid:
            matches.append((session, decision))
            matched_ids.add(pid)
@

An exact ID match collapses the set to that one paper (it may still span several
sessions, which is the legitimate multi-trail case).  Only when there is no
exact match \emph{and} the substring reached more than one paper do we treat the
query as ambiguous.

<<resolve the matches to a single paper>>=
if paper_id in matched_ids:
    matches = [(s, d) for s, d in matches if d.paper.id == paper_id]
elif len(matched_ids) > 1:
    if output_format == "json":
        print("[]")
    else:
        console.print(
            f"[red]Ambiguous paper ID '{escape(paper_id)}' matches "
            f"{len(matched_ids)} papers; re-run with a full ID:[/red]"
        )
        for pid in sorted(matched_ids):
            console.print(f"[red]  {escape(pid)}[/red]")
    raise typer.Exit(1)
@

Each block prints the reproducible command on the first line and the session
plus research-question context on a second, indented to line up under the
command.  The session name and question are wrapped in double quotes for
readability, so a double quote inside either would unbalance the line; we escape
those.  We also collapse any internal whitespace in the research question so a
question entered across several lines still renders as one context line, and we
omit the question entirely when the session never recorded one.

<<print FOUND-VIA blocks>>=
blocks = []
for record in records:
    session_name = record["session"].replace('"', '\\"')
    context = f'(session "{session_name}"'
    research = record["research_context"]
    if research:
        question = " ".join(research.split()).replace('"', '\\"')
        context += f'; research question: "{question}"'
    context += ")"
    blocks.append(
        f"{FOUND_VIA_PREFIX}{record['command']}\n"
        f"{' ' * len(FOUND_VIA_PREFIX)}{context}"
    )
print("\n\n".join(blocks))
<<constants>>=
FOUND_VIA_PREFIX = "FOUND-VIA: "
@

