\subsection{Provenance-stub BibTeX formatter}

When a written claim is backed by a reference, the citation should travel with
a record of \emph{how it was found}, \emph{why it was picked}, and the
\emph{verbatim quote} that supports the claim.  The most error-prone part of
that record is the [[FOUND-VIA]] line --- a reproducible [[scholar search]]
command --- and that is exactly the part the tool already knows.  This
formatter emits each entry wrapped in a provenance comment block with
[[FOUND-VIA]] pre-filled and the judgement fields left as [[TODO]]
placeholders for the author to complete.

The wrinkle is that by the time results reach a formatter they have passed
through [[SearchResult.merge]], which joins the per-provider queries with
[[" | "]] (every segment is identical, since one query is sent to all
providers) and joins the provider names with [[", "]].  To rebuild a command
the reader can actually run, we undo both joins: collapse the duplicated query
segments back to one query and re-emit a single [[-p]] flag per provider.

Free-text arguments --- the query and the [[--venue]] value --- may contain a
space, a quote, or a shell metacharacter, and the whole point of the line is
that the reader can paste it into a shell and re-run it.  Hand-rolled
double-quoting is not enough: inside double quotes a shell still expands [[$]]
and backticks, so a query like [[term $(rm -rf x)]] would \emph{execute} on
paste rather than be searched for.  We therefore delegate to [[shlex.quote]],
which renders any string as a single shell token (single-quoted when needed,
left bare when already safe) with no metacharacter left live.  Both arguments
share this one [[_quote_arg]] helper so the two call sites cannot drift in how
they escape.

<<formatter classes>>=
def _quote_arg(value: str) -> str:
    """Return ``value`` quoted as a single safe shell token."""
    return shlex.quote(value)
@

The filters and limit turn back into the flags that produced them.  We take
them as a plain dict and an integer rather than off a result object, because two
callers reconstruct flags from different sources: the [[bibtex+prov]] formatter
reads them off a live [[SearchResult]], while [[prov found-via]] reads them off
a stored [[SearchRecord]].  The [[--limit]] is shown only when it differs from
the default, and the unlimited sentinel maps back to the [[0]] the user actually
typed rather than the large internal cap.  Output and enrichment options such as
[[--enrich]] are deliberately omitted: they change how results are displayed or
augmented, not \emph{which} papers match, so they are irrelevant to reproducing
the found set.

<<formatter classes>>=
def _search_flags(filters: dict | None, limit: int | None) -> list[str]:
    """Reconstruct the filter and limit CLI flags from a filters dict."""
    flags = []
    filters = filters or {}
    if filters.get("year"):
        flags.append(f"--year {filters['year']}")
    if filters.get("open_access"):
        flags.append("--open-access")
    if filters.get("venue"):
        flags.append(f"--venue {_quote_arg(filters['venue'])}")
    if filters.get("min_citations") is not None:
        flags.append(f"--min-citations {filters['min_citations']}")
    for pub_type in filters.get("pub_types") or []:
        flags.append(f"--type {pub_type}")
    if limit is not None and limit != DEFAULT_SEARCH_LIMIT:
        shown = 0 if limit >= UNLIMITED_PROVIDER_LIMIT else limit
        flags.append(f"--limit {shown}")
    return flags
@

With the helpers in place, [[_found_via]] is just the query, the [[-p]] flags,
and the reconstructed filter flags joined into one command:

<<formatter classes>>=
def _found_via(result) -> str:
    """Reconstruct the ``scholar search`` command behind a result.

    Undoes the ``" | "`` query join and ``", "`` provider join introduced
    by ``SearchResult.merge``, then re-emits the filter and limit flags, so
    the command reproduces the original search.
    """
    queries = list(dict.fromkeys(result.query.split(" | ")))
    query = " | ".join(queries)
    providers = [p.strip() for p in result.provider.split(",") if p.strip()]
    parts = [f"scholar search {_quote_arg(query)}"]
    parts += [f"-p {p}" for p in providers]
    parts += _search_flags(result.filters, result.limit)
    return " ".join(parts)
@

Each paper is preceded by the comment block and then the reused entry.  The
[[DATE]] comes from the search timestamp already carried on the result rather
than the wall clock: it records when the search that surfaced the paper
actually ran, and it keeps the output deterministic under test.

<<formatter classes>>=
class BibTeXProvFormatter(BibTeXFormatter):
    """Output BibTeX entries wrapped in a provenance comment block."""

    def format(self, results: list) -> None:
        """Output results as provenance-stub BibTeX."""
        for result in results:
            found_via = _found_via(result)
            date = result.timestamp.split("T")[0] if result.timestamp else ""
            for paper in result.papers:
                print(f"% === provenance: {_bibtex_key(paper)} ===")
                print("% CLAIM: TODO")
                print(f"% FOUND-VIA: {found_via}")
                print("% PICKED: TODO")
                print("% QUOTE (<loc>): TODO")
                print("% VERIFIED: TODO")
                print(f"% DATE: {date}")
                print(self._format_paper(paper))
                print()
@

