\section{Round-Tripping Provenance to Notes}
\label{sec:prov-roundtrip}

The provenance block above a BibTeX entry is the \emph{authoritative} record of
why a citation was chosen, but [[scholar notes]] keeps its own per-paper
annotations.  Reconciling the two stores by hand is error-prone, so we
round-trip them: [[scholar prov import]] reads the [[.bib]] comment blocks into
notes, and [[scholar prov export]] writes the notes' blocks back above the
entries.  This section builds that round-trip from the inside out --- the note
representation first, then the [[.bib]] parser, then the two commands --- because
each command is just glue over the layers below it.

Three facts shape the design.  First, the two stores key papers differently:
notes use [[Paper.id]] (a DOI, or a hash of title and first-author surname),
while a [[.bib]] block is headed by a \emph{bibkey} (surname plus year).  We
bridge them by reconstructing a [[Paper]] from each entry and letting the notes
layer derive the id --- the DOI is the reliable join, the hash a best-effort
fallback.  Second, one paper can back \emph{several} claims, so a note holds a
\emph{list} of provenance blocks and [[import]] merges them by [[CLAIM]] text
(re-importing never duplicates).  Third, a note also carries the user's own
prose, which must never leak into the [[.bib]] nor be clobbered by an import; we
make provenance blocks self-delimiting so everything else can be preserved
verbatim.

We parse the [[.bib]] ourselves rather than pulling in a BibTeX library: the
provenance lines are \emph{comments} that any such library discards, so it would
only help extract a handful of entry fields --- while the load-bearing work
(associating each comment region with its entry and tracking line spans so an
in-place rewrite does not reformat the file) is ours regardless.  The input is
the format we emit, so a small line scanner is both sufficient and clearer.

\subsection{Recognising a provenance block}

A note is parsed into a sequence of \emph{segments}: opaque personal text, or a
provenance block.  A block is announced by a sentinel header
[[=== provenance: key ===]] and continues through its recognised
[[FIELD: value]] lines; the first line that is neither ends the block.  Anchoring
recognition on the sentinel (rather than guessing from field-like text) is what
lets us treat everything outside a block as personal and keep it untouched.

<<constants>>=
PROV_FIELD_NAMES = {
    "CLAIM", "FOUND-VIA", "PICKED", "QUOTE", "VERIFIED", "DATE",
}
PROV_HEADER_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*===\s*provenance:\s*(.*?)\s*===\s*$")
BIB_ENTRY_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*@\w+\s*\{\s*([^,\s]+)\s*,")
@

A field line is recognised by the token before its first colon, so a value that
itself contains a colon (a [[FOUND-VIA]] command, say) does not confuse the
match.  The [[QUOTE (<loc>)]] field keeps its location in the name, so we compare
only the first whitespace-delimited token against the known set.

<<formatter classes>>=
def _is_prov_field_line(line: str) -> bool:
    """Return True if ``line`` is a recognised ``FIELD: value`` line."""
    name, sep, _ = line.partition(":")
    if not sep:
        return False
    token = name.strip().split(" ", 1)[0]
    return token in PROV_FIELD_NAMES
@

When the blocks arrive from a [[.bib]] they are prefixed with a comment marker.
We strip a leading [[%]] and at most one following space so the inner text
matches the note representation exactly.

<<formatter classes>>=
def _strip_comment_prefix(line: str) -> str:
    """Strip a leading ``%`` (and one space) from a comment line."""
    return re.sub(r"^\s*%\s?", "", line)
@

\subsection{Splitting a note into segments}

The splitter walks the note line by line, accumulating personal lines until a
sentinel header opens a block, then collecting that block's field lines until a
non-field line closes it.  Keeping personal runs as one opaque string (rather
than per-line) means we can re-emit them byte-for-byte, which is the guarantee
that an import never disturbs the user's prose.

<<formatter classes>>=
def _split_note_segments(note_body: str) -> list:
    """Split a note body into ordered personal/provenance segments.

    Returns a list of ``("personal", text)`` and ``("prov", (key, fields))``
    tuples, in document order.
    """
    segments: list = []
    personal: list = []
    fields = None
    key = None

    def flush_personal():
        if personal:
            segments.append(("personal", "\n".join(personal)))
            personal.clear()

    def flush_block():
        nonlocal fields, key
        if fields is not None:
            segments.append(("prov", (key, fields)))
            fields = None
            key = None

    for line in note_body.split("\n"):
        header = PROV_HEADER_RE.match(line)
        if header:
            flush_personal()
            flush_block()
            fields = {}
            key = header.group(1)
        elif fields is not None and _is_prov_field_line(line):
            name, _, value = line.partition(":")
            fields[name.strip()] = value.strip()
        else:
            flush_block()
            personal.append(line)

    flush_personal()
    flush_block()
    return segments
@

The inverse re-serialises segments back to a note body.  Personal segments are
emitted verbatim; a block becomes its header followed by its fields in stored
order.  Splitting then rendering is the identity on a canonically-formatted note
--- which is what makes a note-to-note round-trip lossless --- modulo two
normalisations: a field value is stripped of surrounding whitespace, and because
a block's fields are a [[dict]], two lines with the \emph{same} field name
collapse to the last (so multiple quotes from one source must carry distinct
locations, as in [[QUOTE (p.3)]] versus [[QUOTE (p.7)]]).

<<formatter classes>>=
def _render_note_segments(segments: list) -> str:
    """Re-serialise note segments into a note body (inverse of split)."""
    parts = []
    for kind, payload in segments:
        if kind == "personal":
            parts.append(payload)
        else:
            key, fields = payload
            lines = [f"=== provenance: {key} ==="]
            lines += [f"{name}: {value}" for name, value in fields.items()]
            parts.append("\n".join(lines))
    return "\n".join(parts)
@

Let's verify a note mixing personal prose with a block round-trips unchanged,
and that the parts are recovered as expected:

<<test functions>>=
class TestProvSegments:
    """Tests for note segment parsing and rendering."""

    def test_round_trips_personal_and_block(self):
        """Splitting then rendering reproduces the note verbatim."""
        body = (
            "Cited in chapter 3.\n"
            "=== provenance: alice2024 ===\n"
            "CLAIM: foo\n"
            "DATE: 2026-06-25"
        )
        segments = _split_note_segments(body)
        assert segments[0] == ("personal", "Cited in chapter 3.")
        assert segments[1][0] == "prov"
        assert segments[1][1][0] == "alice2024"
        assert segments[1][1][1]["CLAIM"] == "foo"
        assert _render_note_segments(segments) == body

    def test_personal_text_without_block(self):
        """A note with no block is a single personal segment."""
        segments = _split_note_segments("just a thought")
        assert segments == [("personal", "just a thought")]
@

\subsection{Merging provenance blocks by claim}

Importing must add new claims without duplicating ones already stored and
without touching personal segments.  We key blocks by their [[CLAIM]] text: an
incoming block whose claim matches an existing block replaces it in place;
otherwise it is appended.  Matching by claim (not by position) is what makes
repeated imports idempotent even as the [[.bib]] grows.

<<formatter classes>>=
def _merge_prov(segments: list, incoming: list) -> list:
    """Merge incoming ``(key, fields)`` blocks into ``segments`` by CLAIM."""
    merged = list(segments)
    for key, fields in incoming:
        claim = (fields.get("CLAIM") or "").strip()
        for index, (kind, payload) in enumerate(merged):
            if kind != "prov":
                continue
            if (payload[1].get("CLAIM") or "").strip() == claim:
                merged[index] = ("prov", (key, fields))
                break
        else:
            merged.append(("prov", (key, fields)))
    return merged
@

We verify that a new claim is appended while a matching claim is updated in
place, leaving personal text alone:

<<test functions>>=
class TestProvMerge:
    """Tests for claim-keyed provenance merging."""

    def test_appends_new_claim_keeps_personal(self):
        """A new claim is appended; personal segments survive."""
        segments = [("personal", "my note")]
        merged = _merge_prov(segments, [("k", {"CLAIM": "new"})])
        assert merged[0] == ("personal", "my note")
        assert merged[1] == ("prov", ("k", {"CLAIM": "new"}))

    def test_updates_matching_claim_in_place(self):
        """A block with a known claim replaces the stored one."""
        segments = [("prov", ("old", {"CLAIM": "c", "DATE": "1"}))]
        merged = _merge_prov(segments, [("new", {"CLAIM": "c", "DATE": "2"})])
        assert len(merged) == 1
        assert merged[0][1][1]["DATE"] == "2"
@

\subsection{Parsing a BibTeX file}

We scan the file once, remembering the start of any contiguous run of comment
lines so that when an entry begins we can attach the block(s) directly above it.
A blank or non-comment line resets that memory, so only comments \emph{immediately}
above an entry count as its provenance --- which matches how the formatter emits
them.  The entry's extent is found by brace depth.

<<formatter classes>>=
@dataclass
class ProvEntry:
    """A parsed BibTeX entry with its provenance comment blocks."""

    key: str
    fields: dict
    prov_blocks: list
    comment_start: "int | None"
    entry_start: int
    entry_end: int
@

An entry ends on the line where its brace depth returns to zero.  We only start
counting once a brace has been seen, so a header line such as
[[@article{key,]] is handled the same as a multi-line body.

<<formatter classes>>=
def _entry_end(lines: list, start: int) -> int:
    """Return the index of the line that closes the entry at ``start``."""
    depth = 0
    seen = False
    for index in range(start, len(lines)):
        depth += lines[index].count("{") - lines[index].count("}")
        if "{" in lines[index]:
            seen = True
        if seen and depth <= 0:
            return index
    return len(lines) - 1
@

Field extraction is deliberately narrow: we only need the four fields that
identify a paper, and our own entries write one [[name = {value}]] (or quoted)
field per line.  A line-oriented match avoids mistaking an [[=]] inside a value
for a field assignment.  It assumes each field value is balanced and on one
line --- which the formatter guarantees --- so a value spanning lines or a
literal unbalanced brace is out of scope.

We strip the remaining [[{]] and [[}]] from each value before storing it.  A
[[.bib]] often brace-protects capitalisation, as in [[title = {The {LaTeX}
Companion}]]; left in, those braces would make the title hash to a different
[[Paper.id]] than the brace-free title every provider stores, so an [[import]]
would orphan the paper into a fresh note instead of updating its existing one.
Unwrapping the braces is what lets the no-DOI hash key line up with notes
created elsewhere.

<<formatter classes>>=
def _extract_bib_fields(entry_text: str) -> dict:
    """Pull ``name = {value}`` / ``name = "value"`` fields from an entry."""
    brace = re.compile(r'^\s*(\w+)\s*=\s*\{(.*)\}\s*,?\s*$')
    quote = re.compile(r'^\s*(\w+)\s*=\s*"(.*)"\s*,?\s*$')
    fields = {}
    for line in entry_text.split("\n"):
        match = brace.match(line) or quote.match(line)
        if match:
            value = match.group(2).replace("{", "").replace("}", "")
            fields[match.group(1).lower()] = value
    return fields
@

BibTeX author fields use [[Last, First]] order joined by [[ and ]].  We
normalise each name to [[First Last]] so the reconstructed [[Paper]] hashes to
the same id the rest of the tool would compute for it.

<<formatter classes>>=
def _split_bib_authors(field: str) -> list:
    """Split a BibTeX ``author`` field into ``First Last`` names."""
    authors = []
    for raw in field.split(" and "):
        raw = raw.strip()
        if not raw:
            continue
        if "," in raw:
            last, first = raw.split(",", 1)
            authors.append(f"{first.strip()} {last.strip()}".strip())
        else:
            authors.append(raw)
    return authors
@

From the fields we build a minimal [[Paper]] --- enough for the notes layer to
derive its id.  We import [[Paper]] lazily so this module keeps its existing
import graph.

<<formatter classes>>=
def _paper_from_entry(fields: dict):
    """Build a minimal Paper from entry fields, for note keying only."""
    from scholar.scholar import Paper

    year_raw = fields.get("year", "")
    year = int(year_raw) if year_raw.isdigit() else None
    return Paper(
        title=fields.get("title", ""),
        authors=_split_bib_authors(fields.get("author", "")),
        year=year,
        doi=fields.get("doi"),
    )
@

A comment region may stack several blocks.  We strip the comment markers and
reuse the note splitter, keeping only its provenance segments --- so any stray
non-provenance comment is simply ignored.

<<formatter classes>>=
def _comment_prov_blocks(comment_lines: list) -> list:
    """Extract provenance ``(key, fields)`` blocks from comment lines."""
    stripped = "\n".join(
        _strip_comment_prefix(line) for line in comment_lines
    )
    return [
        payload
        for kind, payload in _split_note_segments(stripped)
        if kind == "prov"
    ]
@

The export rewriter needs the complement: the comment lines that are \emph{not}
part of a provenance block.  A user may keep an ordinary [[%]] note above an
entry, and replacing the provenance region must not take that with it.  We
re-walk the run at the raw-line level --- dropping a header and its field lines,
keeping everything else --- so a personal comment survives an export.

<<formatter classes>>=
def _kept_comment_lines(comment_lines: list) -> list:
    """Return the comment lines that are not part of a provenance block."""
    kept = []
    in_block = False
    for line in comment_lines:
        stripped = _strip_comment_prefix(line)
        if PROV_HEADER_RE.match(stripped):
            in_block = True
            continue
        if in_block and _is_prov_field_line(stripped):
            continue
        in_block = False
        kept.append(line)
    return kept
@

The scanner ties these together: it tracks the pending comment run, and on each
entry records the bibkey, the identifying fields, the attached blocks, and the
line spans the rewriter will need.

<<formatter classes>>=
def _parse_bib_entries(text: str) -> list:
    """Parse a ``.bib`` into entries with their provenance comment blocks."""
    lines = text.split("\n")
    entries = []
    comment_start = None
    index = 0
    while index < len(lines):
        line = lines[index]
        if line.strip().startswith("%"):
            if comment_start is None:
                comment_start = index
            index += 1
            continue
        match = BIB_ENTRY_RE.match(line)
        if match:
            entry_end = _entry_end(lines, index)
            entry_text = "\n".join(lines[index:entry_end + 1])
            blocks = (
                _comment_prov_blocks(lines[comment_start:index])
                if comment_start is not None else []
            )
            entries.append(ProvEntry(
                key=match.group(1),
                fields=_extract_bib_fields(entry_text),
                prov_blocks=blocks,
                comment_start=comment_start,
                entry_start=index,
                entry_end=entry_end,
            ))
            comment_start = None
            index = entry_end + 1
            continue
        comment_start = None
        index += 1
    return entries
@

Let's verify the scanner attaches a stacked pair of blocks to the entry below
them and recovers the entry's fields:

<<test functions>>=
class TestProvBibParse:
    """Tests for parsing provenance blocks out of a .bib file."""

    def test_parses_stacked_blocks_and_fields(self):
