\section{The Provenance Round-Trip Commands}

With the layers in place, the commands are thin.  They attach to the [[prov]]
group introduced in \cref{sec:prov-commands} rather than defining a second
one---a single [[prov_app]] keeps [[found-via]], [[import]], and [[export]]
under one application.

\subsection{Importing blocks into notes}

[[import]] reads each entry's blocks, merges them into the paper's note by
claim, and saves the result.  Reading the existing note first --- and merging
rather than overwriting --- is what preserves both prior claims and the user's
own prose.  Entries without a block are skipped so we never create an empty
note.

<<prov command>>=
@prov_app.command("import")
def prov_import(
    bib_file: Annotated[
        Path,
        typer.Argument(help="BibTeX file to read provenance blocks from."),
    ],
) -> None:
    """
    Import provenance comment blocks from a .bib file into notes.

    Each entry's block(s) are merged into that paper's note, keyed by
    CLAIM so re-importing never duplicates. Personal note text is kept.
    """
    from scholar.notes import get_note, save_note

    console = Console()
    if not bib_file.exists():
        console.print(f"[red]File not found: {escape(str(bib_file))}[/red]")
        raise typer.Exit(1)

    entries = _parse_bib_entries(bib_file.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    count = 0
    for entry in entries:
        if not entry.prov_blocks:
            continue
        paper = _paper_from_entry(entry.fields)
        existing = get_note(paper)
        segments = (
            _split_note_segments(existing.note) if existing else []
        )
        merged = _merge_prov(segments, entry.prov_blocks)
        save_note(paper, _render_note_segments(merged))
        count += 1

    noun = "entry" if count == 1 else "entries"
    console.print(
        f"[green]Imported provenance for {count} {noun}[/green]"
    )
@

